168
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:06,960
Here we see X-ray CT, which you are

169
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:08,720
already familiar with from earlier

170
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:10,319
lectures. Let me give you a quick

171
00:06:08,720 --> 00:06:12,479
review.

172
00:06:10,319 --> 00:06:15,759
In conventional CT, the system typically

173
00:06:12,479 --> 00:06:17,360
uses an energy integrating detector.

174
00:06:15,759 --> 00:06:19,280
This means that all of the incoming

175
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:20,880
X-ray photons are combined and the

176
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:23,520
result is an averaged measurement over

177
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:25,360
the entire X-ray spectrum. That gives us

178
00:06:23,520 --> 00:06:27,199
the grayscale images you know well where

179
00:06:25,360 --> 00:06:28,720
tissues appear in shades from black to

180
00:06:27,199 --> 00:06:30,720
white.

181
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:33,199
Now with more advanced technology, we

182
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:35,280
can use photon counting detectors. These

183
00:06:33,199 --> 00:06:36,880
detectors are energy sensitive which

184
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:38,400
means they can record not only the

185
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:40,160
number of photons but also their

186
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:42,479
energies.

187
00:06:40,160 --> 00:06:44,639
By sorting the detected photons into

188
00:06:42,479 --> 00:06:47,280
many energy bins, we can see the full

189
00:06:44,639 --> 00:06:49,280
X-ray spectrum, including characteristic

190
00:06:47,280 --> 00:06:51,280
features called K edges for different

191
00:06:49,280 --> 00:06:53,199
materials.

192
00:06:51,280 --> 00:06:55,520
This approach allows us to separate and

193
00:06:53,199 --> 00:06:57,680
identify materials such as gold, iodine,

194
00:06:55,520 --> 00:06:59,759
calcium, and fat. In other words,

195
00:06:57,680 --> 00:07:01,680
instead of just a grayscale image, we

196
00:06:59,759 --> 00:07:03,759
can create color-coded images that

197
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:05,919
reflect the molecular composition of the

198
00:07:03,759 --> 00:07:10,000
tissue or phantom. This is called

199
00:07:05,919 --> 00:07:12,160
spectral or molecular kinetic.

200
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,840
So with photon counting CT we move

201
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:15,520
beyond structural imaging into the

202
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:17,759
possibility of functional and molecular

203
00:07:15,520 --> 00:07:20,919
imaging. A very exciting direction for

204
00:07:17,759 --> 00:07:20,919
the future.

205
00:07:22,319 --> 00:07:25,680
Today we move into a very different

206
00:07:23,919 --> 00:07:27,840
story. The basic idea of nuclear

207
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:29,599
imaging. This principle is absolutely

208
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:31,440
fundamental.

209
00:07:29,599 --> 00:07:33,599
Just like in X-ray imaging we are still

210
00:07:31,440 --> 00:07:35,520
working with radiation. But here's the

211
00:07:33,599 --> 00:07:37,680
key difference. In nuclear imaging, the

212
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:40,160
radiation does not come from an external

213
00:07:37,680 --> 00:07:41,680
source outside the body.

214
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:43,039
Instead, it comes from inside the

215
00:07:41,680 --> 00:07:45,440
patient because we introduce a

216
00:07:43,039 --> 00:07:49,199
radioactive tracer into the body. That

217
00:07:45,440 --> 00:07:50,960
tracer emits gamma rays.

218
00:07:49,199 --> 00:07:53,360
Now, gamma rays and x-rays are very

219
00:07:50,960 --> 00:07:54,800
similar. Their energy ranges overlap,

220
00:07:53,360 --> 00:07:56,080
although gamma rays are usually

221
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:58,080
considered to be slightly more

222
00:07:56,080 --> 00:08:00,160
energetic. Both are forms of

223
00:07:58,080 --> 00:08:02,639
electromagnetic radiation, part of the

224
00:08:00,160 --> 00:08:05,520
same spectrum. Importantly, like X-rays,

225
00:08:02,639 --> 00:08:07,599
gamma rays can penetrate the body.

226
00:08:05,520 --> 00:08:09,280
But if we simply detect gamma rays

227
00:08:07,599 --> 00:08:11,199
without direction, we only get a

228
00:08:09,280 --> 00:08:13,440
collection of signals with no spatial

229
00:08:11,199 --> 00:08:15,599
meaning. To form an image, we need to

230
00:08:13,440 --> 00:08:17,680
know where each photon came from. This

231
00:08:15,599 --> 00:08:19,840
is why we use a columator such as a

232
00:08:17,680 --> 00:08:21,599
pinhole columator or a parallel hole

233
00:08:19,840 --> 00:08:24,240
columator.

234
00:08:21,599 --> 00:08:26,080
The columator ensures that only photons

235
00:08:24,240 --> 00:08:28,080
traveling along certain directions are

236
00:08:26,080 --> 00:08:29,759
recorded, giving us the directional

237
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:31,440
information we need for tomographic

238
00:08:29,759 --> 00:08:33,200
imaging.

239
00:08:31,440 --> 00:08:35,680
After passing through the columator, the

240
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:37,519
gamma rays strike a cintilation crystal.

241
00:08:35,680 --> 00:08:39,599
This crystal converts the high energy

242
00:08:37,519 --> 00:08:41,200
gamma photons into flashes of visible

243
00:08:39,599 --> 00:08:43,120
light.

244
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:44,800
Next, photo multiplier tubes take that

245
00:08:43,120 --> 00:08:46,399
light and convert it into electrical

246
00:08:44,800 --> 00:08:48,320
signals, amplifying the signal

247
00:08:46,399 --> 00:08:49,760
dramatically.

248
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:52,080
Those electrical signals are then

249
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:53,360
digitized and sent to a computer. One

250
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:55,360
measurement gives us a single

251
00:08:53,360 --> 00:08:56,959
projection. By collecting many

252
00:08:55,360 --> 00:09:00,160
projections from different angles, we

253
00:08:56,959 --> 00:09:01,600
can reconstruct a tomographic image.

254
00:09:00,160 --> 00:09:03,760
It's important to understand what the

255
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:05,839
resulting image represents. It is not

256
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:08,080
showing us anatomical structure like CT

257
00:09:05,839 --> 00:09:09,920
does. Instead, it shows the distribution

258
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:11,440
of the radioactive tracer inside the

259
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:12,880
body.

260
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:14,959
That means the image reflects

261
00:09:12,880 --> 00:09:17,200
physiology, the function of tissues

262
00:09:14,959 --> 00:09:18,880
rather than just anatomy. This is the

263
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:22,279
key and very powerful idea behind

264
00:09:18,880 --> 00:09:22,279
nuclear imaging.

265
00:09:23,839 --> 00:09:27,760
This slide further illustrates the

266
00:09:25,600 --> 00:09:29,040
principle of nuclear imaging. Here we

267
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:31,200
see the use of a parallel hole

268
00:09:29,040 --> 00:09:32,720
columator. The purpose of the columator

269
00:09:31,200 --> 00:09:35,839
is to make sure that the signals we

270
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:38,240
detect are aligned with the holes.

271
00:09:35,839 --> 00:09:40,240
That way any photon recorded must have

272
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:41,680
come from directly beneath that hole.

273
00:09:40,240 --> 00:09:43,839
And this gives us the spatial

274
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:46,640
information we need. Without it, we

275
00:09:43,839 --> 00:09:48,560
would not know the photon's origin.

276
00:09:46,640 --> 00:09:50,240
Once the gamma photon passes through the

277
00:09:48,560 --> 00:09:52,480
columator, it hits the scintillation

278
00:09:50,240 --> 00:09:54,959
crystal. The crystal converts that high

279
00:09:52,480 --> 00:09:56,880
energy photon into visible light. Then

280
00:09:54,959 --> 00:09:58,640
the photo multiplier tubes convert the

281
00:09:56,880 --> 00:10:00,320
light into an electrical signal which

282
00:09:58,640 --> 00:10:03,200
can be read by the computer for image

283
00:10:00,320 --> 00:10:04,800
formation.

284
00:10:03,200 --> 00:10:07,040
Now notice what happens after a

285
00:10:04,800 --> 00:10:09,200
radioactive tracer is injected into the

286
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:10,959
bloodstream. The tracer travels through

287
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:12,640
the body and tends to accumulate in

288
00:10:10,959 --> 00:10:14,560
certain locations depending on the

289
00:10:12,640 --> 00:10:16,560
physiology.

290
00:10:14,560 --> 00:10:19,040
In this example for cardiac imaging the

291
00:10:16,560 --> 00:10:20,800
tracer concentrates in the heart muscle.

292
00:10:19,040 --> 00:10:22,480
This allows us to see how it penetrates

293
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:24,000
the cardiac chambers and the muscle

294
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:25,600
wall.

295
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,760
The resulting image reflects the

296
00:10:25,600 --> 00:10:29,680
distribution of the tracer. Since the

297
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:31,760
tracer participates in biochemical

298
00:10:29,680 --> 00:10:33,920
reactions, nuclear imaging provides

299
00:10:31,760 --> 00:10:37,279
insight into physiology, the function of

300
00:10:33,920 --> 00:10:38,959
tissues rather than just anatomy.

301
00:10:37,279 --> 00:10:41,279
This makes nuclear imaging highly

302
00:10:38,959 --> 00:10:43,600
complimentary to X-ray imaging. While

303
00:10:41,279 --> 00:10:45,760
X-ray imaging uses an external source

304
00:10:43,600 --> 00:10:48,079
and shows structure, nuclear imaging

305
00:10:45,760 --> 00:10:50,560
relies on an internal radioactive tracer

306
00:10:48,079 --> 00:10:52,079
and shows biological function. Together,

307
00:10:50,560 --> 00:10:54,959
they give us a much more complete

308
00:10:52,079 --> 00:10:54,959
picture.

309
00:10:56,560 --> 00:11:01,040
The physical principles of nuclear

310
00:10:58,160 --> 00:11:03,200
imaging can be summarized as follows.

311
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:04,640
First, we use an unstable isotope as a

312
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:06,800
tracer molecule, also called a

313
00:11:04,640 --> 00:11:08,560
radionuclide. This tracer can be

314
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:10,480
administered in different ways, most

315
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:12,480
commonly through intravenous injection.

316
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:14,320
But in some cases, it can also be taken

317
00:11:12,480 --> 00:11:17,040
orally, for example, by drinking a small

318
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:18,800
amount of radioactive liquid.

319
00:11:17,040 --> 00:11:20,640
Once the tracer enters the body, it

320
00:11:18,800 --> 00:11:23,279
participates in normal metabolic

321
00:11:20,640 --> 00:11:25,600
processes. As it does so, it emits gamma

322
00:11:23,279 --> 00:11:27,519
rays from inside the body. These gamma

323
00:11:25,600 --> 00:11:29,279
rays are then detected and the resulting

324
00:11:27,519 --> 00:11:31,360
measurements reflect the distribution of

325
00:11:29,279 --> 00:11:33,200
the tracer.

326
00:11:31,360 --> 00:11:35,120
What makes this so powerful is that the

327
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:37,279
signal corresponds to metabolism and

328
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:39,519
physiological function rather than just

329
00:11:37,279 --> 00:11:41,279
anatomical structure. In other words,

330
00:11:39,519 --> 00:11:42,880
nuclear imaging allows us to see how

331
00:11:41,279 --> 00:11:44,399
tissues are working, not just how they

332
00:11:42,880 --> 00:11:46,240
look.

333
00:11:44,399 --> 00:11:48,480
This sets the stage for us to compare

334
00:11:46,240 --> 00:11:50,399
nuclear imaging with X-ray imaging and

335
00:11:48,480 --> 00:11:54,839
to highlight both the similarities and

336
00:11:50,399 --> 00:11:54,839
the important differences between them.

337
00:11:56,160 --> 00:12:00,320
Here we compare X-ray CT with nuclear

338
00:11:58,480 --> 00:12:02,480
imaging by looking at their similarities

339
00:12:00,320 --> 00:12:04,640
and differences.

340
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:06,880
On the left you see a CT scanner and CT

341
00:12:04,640 --> 00:12:08,720
images. On the right is a PET scanner

342
00:12:06,880 --> 00:12:10,639
which represents nuclear imaging.

343
00:12:08,720 --> 00:12:13,040
Although the images shown here may not

344
00:12:10,639 --> 00:12:15,040
be perfectly matched, the idea is clear.

345
00:12:13,040 --> 00:12:17,920
Both modalities use radiation and

346
00:12:15,040 --> 00:12:20,800
produce tomographic images.

347
00:12:17,920 --> 00:12:22,639
Let's begin with the similarities.

348
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:24,800
First, gamma rays and x-rays have

349
00:12:22,639 --> 00:12:26,399
overlapping energy ranges. Their

350
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:28,240
energies are high enough that when

351
00:12:26,399 --> 00:12:29,839
passing through biological tissue, we

352
00:12:28,240 --> 00:12:32,079
can often treat them as traveling in

353
00:12:29,839 --> 00:12:34,000
straight lines. Of course, scattering

354
00:12:32,079 --> 00:12:36,160
occurs, but for image reconstruction, we

355
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:39,440
assume straight line geometry that

356
00:12:36,160 --> 00:12:41,360
simplifies tomographic reconstruction.

357
00:12:39,440 --> 00:12:44,000
Second, the data from both modalities

358
00:12:41,360 --> 00:12:47,440
can be expressed as line integrals. In

359
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:49,600
CT, this is exactly the radon transform.

360
00:12:47,440 --> 00:12:51,120
In nuclear imaging, it is a related but

361
00:12:49,600 --> 00:12:53,920
slightly different mathematical

362
00:12:51,120 --> 00:12:55,920
formulation. Still, both methods involve

363
00:12:53,920 --> 00:12:58,399
line integrals and support tomographic

364
00:12:55,920 --> 00:13:00,800
image reconstruction.

365
00:12:58,399 --> 00:13:02,639
Third, both CT and nuclear imaging

366
00:13:00,800 --> 00:13:04,399
generate two-dimensional slices,

367
00:13:02,639 --> 00:13:06,639
three-dimensional volumes, or even

368
00:13:04,399 --> 00:13:08,880
fourdimensional data sets when dynamics

369
00:13:06,639 --> 00:13:10,560
are included. So in terms of imaging

370
00:13:08,880 --> 00:13:14,399
geometry and reconstruction, they share

371
00:13:10,560 --> 00:13:16,880
a strong common foundation.

372
00:13:14,399 --> 00:13:18,959
Now let's turn to the differences. In

373
00:13:16,880 --> 00:13:21,200
CT, the radiation source is external,

374
00:13:18,959 --> 00:13:23,040
the X-ray tube outside the patient. In

375
00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:25,279
nuclear imaging, the source is internal,

376
00:13:23,040 --> 00:13:27,680
a radioactive tracer introduced into the

377
00:13:25,279 --> 00:13:29,360
bloodstream. This tracer accumulates in

378
00:13:27,680 --> 00:13:31,360
certain regions such as the heart or

379
00:13:29,360 --> 00:13:33,680
tumors, especially malignant tumors with

380
00:13:31,360 --> 00:13:35,279
a high blood supply.

381
00:13:33,680 --> 00:13:38,000
Another difference is the type of

382
00:13:35,279 --> 00:13:39,600
information provided. CT primarily gives

383
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:42,000
anatomical information, structural

384
00:13:39,600 --> 00:13:44,000
details of organs and tissues. Nuclear

385
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,600
imaging by contrast provides functional

386
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:48,399
information showing how tissues are

387
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:50,079
metabolizing or reacting biologically.

388
00:13:48,399 --> 00:13:52,639
This makes nuclear imaging highly

389
00:13:50,079 --> 00:13:54,480
complimentary to CT.

390
00:13:52,639 --> 00:13:57,360
Third, there is a difference in flux and

391
00:13:54,480 --> 00:13:59,600
resolution. CT uses a strong external

392
00:13:57,360 --> 00:14:02,639
X-ray beam producing high photon flux

393
00:13:59,600 --> 00:14:04,480
and therefore high resolution images.

394
00:14:02,639 --> 00:14:06,320
Nuclear imaging uses only a small

395
00:14:04,480 --> 00:14:08,880
injected amount of tracer. So the signal

396
00:14:06,320 --> 00:14:11,279
is weak. The data are noisier and the

397
00:14:08,880 --> 00:14:13,120
spatial resolution is lower. Nuclear

398
00:14:11,279 --> 00:14:15,839
images may not look as sharp as CT

399
00:14:13,120 --> 00:14:18,480
images.

400
00:14:15,839 --> 00:14:20,639
Finally, there is sensitivity. CT

401
00:14:18,480 --> 00:14:22,639
detects differences in tissue density,

402
00:14:20,639 --> 00:14:24,720
but since most tissues are made of light

403
00:14:22,639 --> 00:14:26,720
elements, benign and malignant tumors

404
00:14:24,720 --> 00:14:29,519
can appear very similar until they grow

405
00:14:26,720 --> 00:14:31,760
large enough to change anatomy.

406
00:14:29,519 --> 00:14:34,160
That means CT may detect cancer only at

407
00:14:31,760 --> 00:14:36,639
later stages. Nuclear imaging, however,

408
00:14:34,160 --> 00:14:38,399
is far more sensitive because the tracer

409
00:14:36,639 --> 00:14:40,959
accumulates in areas of abnormal

410
00:14:38,399 --> 00:14:43,519
metabolism. Nuclear imaging can reveal

411
00:14:40,959 --> 00:14:46,399
very small or early stage tumors, often

412
00:14:43,519 --> 00:14:48,320
before structural changes appear.

413
00:14:46,399 --> 00:14:50,240
This sensitivity is one of the greatest

414
00:14:48,320 --> 00:14:52,240
strengths of nuclear imaging, making it

415
00:14:50,240 --> 00:14:55,880
an invaluable tool for early detection

416
00:14:52,240 --> 00:14:55,880
and treatment planning.

417
00:14:57,360 --> 00:15:01,519
CT and nuclear imaging are highly

418
00:14:59,199 --> 00:15:03,360
complimentary. CT provides detailed

419
00:15:01,519 --> 00:15:05,440
anatomical information while nuclear

420
00:15:03,360 --> 00:15:08,000
imaging shows functional and biochemical

421
00:15:05,440 --> 00:15:10,720
processes. So the natural idea is why

422
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:12,240
not combine them? That's exactly what

423
00:15:10,720 --> 00:15:14,480
happened with the development of hybrid

424
00:15:12,240 --> 00:15:16,160
scanners. One of the pioneers in this

425
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:18,399
effort was Simon Cherry who helped

426
00:15:16,160 --> 00:15:20,320
establish PET CT as a practical

427
00:15:18,399 --> 00:15:22,240
technology.

428
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:24,160
The major strength of nuclear imaging is

429
00:15:22,240 --> 00:15:26,240
its ability to label many types of

430
00:15:24,160 --> 00:15:28,320
metabolites. This makes it highly

431
00:15:26,240 --> 00:15:31,199
sensitive for studying biochemical and

432
00:15:28,320 --> 00:15:33,040
physiological processes inside the body.

433
00:15:31,199 --> 00:15:34,880
The limitation of course is that nuclear

434
00:15:33,040 --> 00:15:37,040
imaging by itself has relatively low

435
00:15:34,880 --> 00:15:40,480
resolution. That's why it works best

436
00:15:37,040 --> 00:15:42,720
when combined with CT or MRI.

437
00:15:40,480 --> 00:15:44,320
CT or MRI gives us the anatomical

438
00:15:42,720 --> 00:15:46,639
background, the structural map of

439
00:15:44,320 --> 00:15:48,720
tissues. The nuclear tracer distribution

440
00:15:46,639 --> 00:15:50,160
can then be precisely superimposed on

441
00:15:48,720 --> 00:15:52,240
that background, maximizing the

442
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:54,000
information content. This combination

443
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:56,720
allows us to see both anatomy and

444
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:58,880
function in perfect alignment.

445
00:15:56,720 --> 00:16:01,279
PET CT was the first successful hybrid

446
00:15:58,880 --> 00:16:03,199
scanner. Today, in oncology departments,

447
00:16:01,279 --> 00:16:05,199
it is rare to see a standalone PET

448
00:16:03,199 --> 00:16:07,680
scanner. Almost always, PET is

449
00:16:05,199 --> 00:16:09,360
integrated with CT.

450
00:16:07,680 --> 00:16:11,199
This avoids the problems of image

451
00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:13,360
registration that occur when separate

452
00:16:11,199 --> 00:16:15,120
scanners are used. Moving the patient

453
00:16:13,360 --> 00:16:17,360
between machines can change posture or

454
00:16:15,120 --> 00:16:19,360
positioning, making alignment difficult.

455
00:16:17,360 --> 00:16:21,920
With a single integrated scanner, those

456
00:16:19,360 --> 00:16:24,079
issues are eliminated.

457
00:16:21,920 --> 00:16:25,920
Later, PET MRI was also developed,

458
00:16:24,079 --> 00:16:27,839
bringing together molecular imaging with

459
00:16:25,920 --> 00:16:29,759
the excellent soft tissue contrast of

460
00:16:27,839 --> 00:16:31,279
MRI. And researchers have even

461
00:16:29,759 --> 00:16:34,720
envisioned the possibility of a

462
00:16:31,279 --> 00:16:36,800
trimodality scanner pet and MRI all in

463
00:16:34,720 --> 00:16:38,639
one system that would give us an even

464
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:41,639
more powerful tool for comprehensive

465
00:16:38,639 --> 00:16:41,639
imaging.

466
00:16:43,120 --> 00:16:46,320
We have now finished the general

467
00:16:44,560 --> 00:16:48,720
perspective. So, let's move on to

468
00:16:46,320 --> 00:16:50,639
radioactivity.

469
00:16:48,720 --> 00:16:52,800
Radioactivity is explained in several

470
00:16:50,639 --> 00:16:54,800
sections of the textbook. The reading

471
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:57,120
itself is not especially difficult, but

472
00:16:54,800 --> 00:16:58,639
the terminology can feel confusing,

473
00:16:57,120 --> 00:17:01,199
especially if you don't have a strong

474
00:16:58,639 --> 00:17:02,720
background in chemistry. I've had to go

475
00:17:01,199 --> 00:17:04,480
through these materials multiple times

476
00:17:02,720 --> 00:17:06,720
myself.

477
00:17:04,480 --> 00:17:08,559
Over time it becomes clearer. What we

478
00:17:06,720 --> 00:17:11,679
are really dealing with are physical and

479
00:17:08,559 --> 00:17:14,720
chemical phenomena at the atomic level.

480
00:17:11,679 --> 00:17:16,559
So why does radioactivity occur?

481
00:17:14,720 --> 00:17:19,039
The reason is that certain isotopes are

482
00:17:16,559 --> 00:17:21,199
unstable. These isotopes undergo decay

483
00:17:19,039 --> 00:17:23,039
releasing radiation in the process. The

484
00:17:21,199 --> 00:17:26,079
decay can follow different mechanisms

485
00:17:23,039 --> 00:17:28,240
which we will study in detail.

486
00:17:26,079 --> 00:17:30,720
Importantly radioactive decay follows an

487
00:17:28,240 --> 00:17:32,640
exponential curve. This behavior can be

488
00:17:30,720 --> 00:17:35,200
modeled mathematically using ordinary

489
00:17:32,640 --> 00:17:36,880
differential equations.

490
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:39,120
Once you see the formulation, it's not

491
00:17:36,880 --> 00:17:40,880
as difficult as it sounds. With some

492
00:17:39,120 --> 00:17:42,400
practice, the mathematics will become

493
00:17:40,880 --> 00:17:44,480
straightforward and it provides a

494
00:17:42,400 --> 00:17:47,480
precise way to describe radioactive

495
00:17:44,480 --> 00:17:47,480
processes.

496
00:17:49,120 --> 00:17:52,880
This slide is mainly a review of basic

497
00:17:51,120 --> 00:17:55,280
nuclear physics concepts. The atomic

498
00:17:52,880 --> 00:17:57,440
number and the mass number.

499
00:17:55,280 --> 00:17:59,280
The atomic number denoted by Z is simply

500
00:17:57,440 --> 00:18:01,679
the number of protons in the nucleus of

501
00:17:59,280 --> 00:18:04,000
an atom. The mass number usually written

502
00:18:01,679 --> 00:18:05,679
as A is the total number of nucleons

503
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:08,000
meaning the number of protons plus the

504
00:18:05,679 --> 00:18:09,919
number of neutrons. In most cases the

505
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:12,080
atomic weight is approximately equal to

506
00:18:09,919 --> 00:18:13,919
this mass number.

507
00:18:12,080 --> 00:18:16,320
For example, in helium the atomic number

508
00:18:13,919 --> 00:18:18,320
is two because there are two protons.

509
00:18:16,320 --> 00:18:21,280
The mass number is four because helium

510
00:18:18,320 --> 00:18:23,039
has two protons and two neutrons.

511
00:18:21,280 --> 00:18:24,880
When an atom is neutral, the number of

512
00:18:23,039 --> 00:18:26,559
electrons surrounding the nucleus

513
00:18:24,880 --> 00:18:30,480
matches the number of protons in the

514
00:18:26,559 --> 00:18:32,240
nucleus. This keeps the charge balanced.

515
00:18:30,480 --> 00:18:34,080
Now, once we understand atomic number

516
00:18:32,240 --> 00:18:37,720
and mass number, we can define what

517
00:18:34,080 --> 00:18:37,720
isotopes are.

518
00:18:38,960 --> 00:18:44,000
Now, let's talk about isotopes.

519
00:18:41,840 --> 00:18:45,919
Isotopes of a chemical element all share

520
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:48,320
the same atomic number, meaning they

521
00:18:45,919 --> 00:18:49,919
have the same number of protons. But

522
00:18:48,320 --> 00:18:51,520
they differ in their mass numbers

523
00:18:49,919 --> 00:18:53,600
because the number of neutrons is

524
00:18:51,520 --> 00:18:54,960
different. So isotopes are the same

525
00:18:53,600 --> 00:18:56,640
element but with different neutron

526
00:18:54,960 --> 00:18:58,720
counts.

527
00:18:56,640 --> 00:19:00,400
Inside the nucleus, protons and neutrons

528
00:18:58,720 --> 00:19:02,640
are held together by the strong nuclear

529
00:19:00,400 --> 00:19:04,640
force. When the balance between protons

530
00:19:02,640 --> 00:19:07,440
and neutrons is within a stable range,

531
00:19:04,640 --> 00:19:09,039
the nucleus remains stable.

532
00:19:07,440 --> 00:19:11,120
But if the configuration becomes

533
00:19:09,039 --> 00:19:13,200
unbalanced, for example, when there are

534
00:19:11,120 --> 00:19:15,440
too many neutrons or too many protons

535
00:19:13,200 --> 00:19:17,520
relative to each other, the nucleus can

536
00:19:15,440 --> 00:19:19,280
become unstable.

537
00:19:17,520 --> 00:19:21,600
An unstable nucleus will eventually

538
00:19:19,280 --> 00:19:23,440
change into a more stable state. This

539
00:19:21,600 --> 00:19:25,679
happens through radioactive decay, which

540
00:19:23,440 --> 00:19:27,440
follows an exponential curve. We'll

541
00:19:25,679 --> 00:19:29,360
explore that decay law in more detail

542
00:19:27,440 --> 00:19:32,360
and even derive it mathematically later

543
00:19:29,360 --> 00:19:32,360
on.

544
00:19:33,679 --> 00:19:37,679
Radioactive isotopes can emit gamma

545
00:19:35,520 --> 00:19:39,440
rays. Let's look more closely at what

546
00:19:37,679 --> 00:19:41,520
that means.

547
00:19:39,440 --> 00:19:43,360
On the electromagnetic spectrum, X-rays

548
00:19:41,520 --> 00:19:46,000
and gamma rays actually overlap in terms

549
00:19:43,360 --> 00:19:48,000
of energy. X-rays typically fall within

550
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:50,400
a certain energy range, while gamma rays

551
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:52,400
can extend both lower and higher. In

552
00:19:50,400 --> 00:19:54,400
medical imaging, gamma rays are often in

553
00:19:52,400 --> 00:19:56,080
the range of hundreds of kilo electron

554
00:19:54,400 --> 00:19:58,960
volts.

555
00:19:56,080 --> 00:20:00,799
For example, 511 kilo electron volts is

556
00:19:58,960 --> 00:20:03,600
a key energy level used in posetron

557
00:20:00,799 --> 00:20:05,600
emission tomography or PET. At lower

558
00:20:03,600 --> 00:20:07,600
energies, gamma rays are used in single

559
00:20:05,600 --> 00:20:09,280
photon emission computed tomography or

560
00:20:07,600 --> 00:20:11,360
spec.

561
00:20:09,280 --> 00:20:13,360
So in terms of energy, x-rays and gamma

562
00:20:11,360 --> 00:20:14,720
rays are quite similar. The main

563
00:20:13,360 --> 00:20:16,960
difference lies in how they are

564
00:20:14,720 --> 00:20:18,880
generated. X-rays are produced by

565
00:20:16,960 --> 00:20:20,559
electron interactions or for instance

566
00:20:18,880 --> 00:20:22,720
when high energy electrons strike a

567
00:20:20,559 --> 00:20:24,559
tungsten target generating X-rays in the

568
00:20:22,720 --> 00:20:26,559
process.

569
00:20:24,559 --> 00:20:29,200
In contrast, gamma rays are produced by

570
00:20:26,559 --> 00:20:31,520
transitions inside the nucleus itself.

571
00:20:29,200 --> 00:20:33,440
When an unstable nucleus changes from a

572
00:20:31,520 --> 00:20:35,679
higher energy state to a lower energy

573
00:20:33,440 --> 00:20:38,400
state, it releases that excess energy in

574
00:20:35,679 --> 00:20:40,480
the form of a gamma photon.

575
00:20:38,400 --> 00:20:42,000
This distinction is important. X-rays

576
00:20:40,480 --> 00:20:44,240
come from interactions involving

577
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:46,960
electrons while gamma rays come directly

578
00:20:44,240 --> 00:20:49,039
from nuclear transitions.

579
00:20:46,960 --> 00:20:50,720
Both are powerful tools in imaging. And

580
00:20:49,039 --> 00:20:52,880
as we move forward, we'll see in more

581
00:20:50,720 --> 00:20:55,880
detail how gamma rays are generated and

582
00:20:52,880 --> 00:20:55,880
used.

583
00:20:57,360 --> 00:21:01,360
Radioactive isotopes can decay through

584
00:20:59,520 --> 00:21:04,320
several different mechanisms and it's

585
00:21:01,360 --> 00:21:06,240
important to understand the main ones

586
00:21:04,320 --> 00:21:08,880
in your textbook. These are grouped into

587
00:21:06,240 --> 00:21:10,880
four basic types. The first is alpha

588
00:21:08,880 --> 00:21:12,880
decay. The second is beta decay which

589
00:21:10,880 --> 00:21:15,360
includes both beta minus and beta plus

590
00:21:12,880 --> 00:21:16,960
or posetron emission. The third is gamma

591
00:21:15,360 --> 00:21:18,640
decay and the fourth is electron

592
00:21:16,960 --> 00:21:20,320
capture.

593
00:21:18,640 --> 00:21:22,799
An easy way to remember them is simply

594
00:21:20,320 --> 00:21:24,720
in order alpha, beta, gamma and then

595
00:21:22,799 --> 00:21:26,559
electron capture. Each of these

596
00:21:24,720 --> 00:21:28,480
processes describes a different way in

597
00:21:26,559 --> 00:21:30,559
which an unstable nucleus transforms

598
00:21:28,480 --> 00:21:33,200
into a more stable state, releasing

599
00:21:30,559 --> 00:21:35,200
radiation in the process.

600
00:21:33,200 --> 00:21:39,240
We'll go into detail about each type of

601
00:21:35,200 --> 00:21:39,240
decay in the following slides.

602
00:21:40,799 --> 00:21:45,679
Let's begin with alpha decay.

603
00:21:43,360 --> 00:21:47,600
In alpha decay, an unstable isotope

604
00:21:45,679 --> 00:21:49,919
transforms into a daughter nucleus and

605
00:21:47,600 --> 00:21:51,840
emits an alpha particle. An alpha

606
00:21:49,919 --> 00:21:53,919
particle is essentially the nucleus of a

607
00:21:51,840 --> 00:21:56,559
helium atom. two protons and two

608
00:21:53,919 --> 00:21:58,640
neutrons bound together.

609
00:21:56,559 --> 00:22:01,280
Alpha particles carry a relatively high

610
00:21:58,640 --> 00:22:03,679
energy, typically in the range of 3 to 7

611
00:22:01,280 --> 00:22:06,080
mega electron volts. Because of this,

612
00:22:03,679 --> 00:22:08,000
they can cause severe tissue damage.

613
00:22:06,080 --> 00:22:09,919
However, they cannot travel far, usually

614
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:11,600
only a few millimeters in biological

615
00:22:09,919 --> 00:22:13,679
tissue.

616
00:22:11,600 --> 00:22:16,240
For this reason, alpha radiation is not

617
00:22:13,679 --> 00:22:18,640
useful for imaging. It doesn't penetrate

618
00:22:16,240 --> 00:22:21,840
deeply enough to form images. Instead,

619
00:22:18,640 --> 00:22:23,520
it has therapeutic applications.

620
00:22:21,840 --> 00:22:25,840
If we can direct alpha emmitting

621
00:22:23,520 --> 00:22:27,840
isotopes precisely to cancer cells, they

622
00:22:25,840 --> 00:22:29,600
can deliver very localized, highly

623
00:22:27,840 --> 00:22:32,640
destructive radiation that kills the

624
00:22:29,600 --> 00:22:34,640
tumor while sparing surrounding tissue.

625
00:22:32,640 --> 00:22:37,039
So while alpha decay is the first

626
00:22:34,640 --> 00:22:39,120
mechanism of radioactive decay we study,

627
00:22:37,039 --> 00:22:42,919
it is more relevant to radiation therapy

628
00:22:39,120 --> 00:22:42,919
than to nuclear imaging.

629
00:22:44,400 --> 00:22:48,799
The second major type of decay is called

630
00:22:46,159 --> 00:22:51,760
beta decay. There are two forms, beta

631
00:22:48,799 --> 00:22:54,000
minus decay and beta plus decay.

632
00:22:51,760 --> 00:22:56,240
In beta minus decay, a neutron inside

633
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:58,480
the nucleus converts into a proton

634
00:22:56,240 --> 00:23:00,640
releasing an electron which we call a

635
00:22:58,480 --> 00:23:03,200
beta minus particle along with an

636
00:23:00,640 --> 00:23:05,520
anti-utrino. This process changes the

637
00:23:03,200 --> 00:23:07,200
nucleus into a different element. Beta

638
00:23:05,520 --> 00:23:08,960
minus particles can be damaging to

639
00:23:07,200 --> 00:23:11,679
tissue and in some cases this type of

640
00:23:08,960 --> 00:23:14,480
decay is used for therapy.

641
00:23:11,679 --> 00:23:16,400
In beta plus decay, a proton in the

642
00:23:14,480 --> 00:23:18,159
nucleus converts into a neutron,

643
00:23:16,400 --> 00:23:20,480
releasing a posetron, which is a

644
00:23:18,159 --> 00:23:22,880
positively charged electron along with a

645
00:23:20,480 --> 00:23:25,200
neutrino. The posetron does not remain

646
00:23:22,880 --> 00:23:27,200
in the body for long.

647
00:23:25,200 --> 00:23:28,960
It quickly encounters a nearby electron

648
00:23:27,200 --> 00:23:31,280
and when the two meet, they annihilate

649
00:23:28,960 --> 00:23:33,679
each other. This annihilation produces a

650
00:23:31,280 --> 00:23:35,440
pair of gamma photons.

651
00:23:33,679 --> 00:23:37,679
These gamma photons do not emit

652
00:23:35,440 --> 00:23:41,120
randomly. They travel in opposite

653
00:23:37,679 --> 00:23:43,280
directions almost exactly 180° apart.

654
00:23:41,120 --> 00:23:46,080
This property is the basis for posetron

655
00:23:43,280 --> 00:23:48,080
emission tomography or PET. By detecting

656
00:23:46,080 --> 00:23:50,480
these pairs of photons and knowing they

657
00:23:48,080 --> 00:23:52,480
travel in straight opposite directions,

658
00:23:50,480 --> 00:23:56,400
we can reconstruct images of where the

659
00:23:52,480 --> 00:23:58,159
tracer accumulated in the body.

660
00:23:56,400 --> 00:24:00,159
Some textbooks separate posetron

661
00:23:58,159 --> 00:24:02,559
emission from beta decay and list it as

662
00:24:00,159 --> 00:24:05,120
a distinct process. Others group it

663
00:24:02,559 --> 00:24:06,960
under beta plus decay.

664
00:24:05,120 --> 00:24:09,120
Either way, the important point is this.

665
00:24:06,960 --> 00:24:11,200
For imaging purposes, beta plus decay

666
00:24:09,120 --> 00:24:13,840
and gamma decay are useful. While alpha

667
00:24:11,200 --> 00:24:15,919
decay and beta minus decay are not pet

668
00:24:13,840 --> 00:24:19,720
in particular relies on beta plus decay

669
00:24:15,919 --> 00:24:19,720
and posetron annihilation.

670
00:24:21,600 --> 00:24:25,600
Posetron emission is another key decay

671
00:24:23,679 --> 00:24:28,000
mechanism and it is especially important

672
00:24:25,600 --> 00:24:29,919
for nuclear imaging.

673
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:31,919
Here's what happens inside an unstable

674
00:24:29,919 --> 00:24:34,240
nucleus. A proton transforms into a

675
00:24:31,919 --> 00:24:36,559
neutron. In the process, the nucleus

676
00:24:34,240 --> 00:24:39,200
emits a posetron which is a positively

677
00:24:36,559 --> 00:24:41,120
charged electron along with a neutrino.

678
00:24:39,200 --> 00:24:42,880
The emitted posetron travels only a

679
00:24:41,120 --> 00:24:44,559
short distance before it encounters an

680
00:24:42,880 --> 00:24:46,400
electron.

681
00:24:44,559 --> 00:24:48,159
Because they carry opposite charges, the

682
00:24:46,400 --> 00:24:50,559
positron and the electron are naturally

683
00:24:48,159 --> 00:24:52,400
attracted to one another.

684
00:24:50,559 --> 00:24:54,720
When they meet, they annihilate each

685
00:24:52,400 --> 00:24:56,640
other. This annihilation produces two

686
00:24:54,720 --> 00:25:00,080
gamma photons, each with an energy of

687
00:24:56,640 --> 00:25:01,840
511 kilo electron volts. These photons

688
00:25:00,080 --> 00:25:05,360
travel in opposite directions almost

689
00:25:01,840 --> 00:25:07,520
exactly 180° apart.

690
00:25:05,360 --> 00:25:10,400
This property is the basis for posetron

691
00:25:07,520 --> 00:25:12,480
emission tomography or PET. By detecting

692
00:25:10,400 --> 00:25:14,400
pairs of gamma photons and knowing they

693
00:25:12,480 --> 00:25:16,080
must lie along the same line, we can

694
00:25:14,400 --> 00:25:18,559
reconstruct the location where the

695
00:25:16,080 --> 00:25:20,080
posetron annihilation occurred. That

696
00:25:18,559 --> 00:25:22,640
gives us an image of the tracer

697
00:25:20,080 --> 00:25:24,799
distribution inside the body.

698
00:25:22,640 --> 00:25:26,960
So compared with alpha and beta minus

699
00:25:24,799 --> 00:25:29,039
decay, posetron emission is extremely

700
00:25:26,960 --> 00:25:30,960
valuable for imaging. It is the physical

701
00:25:29,039 --> 00:25:33,120
principle behind PET, one of the most

702
00:25:30,960 --> 00:25:36,000
important functional imaging modalities

703
00:25:33,120 --> 00:25:36,000
in medicine.

704
00:25:37,840 --> 00:25:42,480
Now let's look at gamma decay.

705
00:25:40,559 --> 00:25:44,400
Gamma decay occurs when a nucleus

706
00:25:42,480 --> 00:25:45,919
changes its internal configuration and

707
00:25:44,400 --> 00:25:48,640
releases the excess energy in the form

708
00:25:45,919 --> 00:25:50,400
of a gamma photon. This process does not

709
00:25:48,640 --> 00:25:52,240
change the number of protons or

710
00:25:50,400 --> 00:25:54,240
neutrons. So the element remains the

711
00:25:52,240 --> 00:25:56,559
same. Only the energy state of the

712
00:25:54,240 --> 00:25:58,799
nucleus changes.

713
00:25:56,559 --> 00:26:01,279
There are two main categories. The first

714
00:25:58,799 --> 00:26:03,200
is immediate gamma decay. In this case,

715
00:26:01,279 --> 00:26:04,960
the nucleus drops from a higher energy

716
00:26:03,200 --> 00:26:08,080
state directly to a lower state and a

717
00:26:04,960 --> 00:26:09,919
gamma photon is released right away.

718
00:26:08,080 --> 00:26:12,000
The second category is more common and

719
00:26:09,919 --> 00:26:13,679
more useful in practice. Here the

720
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:16,080
nucleus does not release the gamma

721
00:26:13,679 --> 00:26:18,000
photon immediately.

722
00:26:16,080 --> 00:26:20,320
Instead, it first enters a metastable

723
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:22,400
state. After a short delay, the gamma

724
00:26:20,320 --> 00:26:25,919
photon is released sometimes along with

725
00:26:22,400 --> 00:26:27,840
other particles such as an anti-utrino.

726
00:26:25,919 --> 00:26:32,080
A classic example of this process is

727
00:26:27,840 --> 00:26:34,320
technium 99M often abbreviated as TC99M.

728
00:26:32,080 --> 00:26:36,320
The M stands for metastable. This

729
00:26:34,320 --> 00:26:37,840
isotope is widely used in nuclear

730
00:26:36,320 --> 00:26:40,240
medicine because of its convenient

731
00:26:37,840 --> 00:26:43,120
half-life and the gamma photons it emits

732
00:26:40,240 --> 00:26:45,200
which are ideal for imaging.

733
00:26:43,120 --> 00:26:47,039
So gamma decay is highly relevant to

734
00:26:45,200 --> 00:26:50,240
nuclear imaging especially in the form

735
00:26:47,039 --> 00:26:52,240
of isotopes like TC99M which remains one

736
00:26:50,240 --> 00:26:55,240
of the most important traces in clinical

737
00:26:52,240 --> 00:26:55,240
practice.

738
00:26:56,799 --> 00:27:00,320
The textbook explains gamma decay in

739
00:26:58,720 --> 00:27:02,880
more detail and it makes an important

740
00:27:00,320 --> 00:27:05,360
point. No radionuclide decays solely by

741
00:27:02,880 --> 00:27:07,200
gamma emission. Instead, gamma rays

742
00:27:05,360 --> 00:27:08,880
appear as part of a decay scheme where

743
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:11,679
an intermediate state called a

744
00:27:08,880 --> 00:27:14,720
metastable state is formed.

745
00:27:11,679 --> 00:27:16,320
A widely used example is technium 99m or

746
00:27:14,720 --> 00:27:18,720
TC9M.

747
00:27:16,320 --> 00:27:21,520
It is produced from malibdinum 99 which

748
00:27:18,720 --> 00:27:24,960
underos beta decay. The daughter nucleus

749
00:27:21,520 --> 00:27:26,720
T99M is metastable. After a half-life of

750
00:27:24,960 --> 00:27:29,120
about 6 hours, it transitions to the

751
00:27:26,720 --> 00:27:31,440
stable form TC99, releasing a gamma

752
00:27:29,120 --> 00:27:34,240
photon in the process.

753
00:27:31,440 --> 00:27:37,600
The gamma photon from TC99M has an

754
00:27:34,240 --> 00:27:39,360
energy of about 140 kilo electron volts.

755
00:27:37,600 --> 00:27:41,760
This energy is ideal for nuclear

756
00:27:39,360 --> 00:27:44,480
medicine imaging. If the photon energy

757
00:27:41,760 --> 00:27:46,480
is too low below about 100 KV, most of

758
00:27:44,480 --> 00:27:48,960
the photons are absorbed in tissue and

759
00:27:46,480 --> 00:27:51,120
never reach the detector.

760
00:27:48,960 --> 00:27:53,440
If the energy is too high above about

761
00:27:51,120 --> 00:27:56,000
200 KV, the photons penetrate the

762
00:27:53,440 --> 00:27:57,600
columator and reduce image quality. That

763
00:27:56,000 --> 00:28:01,360
is why the sweet spot for imaging is

764
00:27:57,600 --> 00:28:03,919
between 100 and 200 KV and TC99M fits

765
00:28:01,360 --> 00:28:06,799
perfectly in this range.

766
00:28:03,919 --> 00:28:08,960
This is why TC9M has become the most

767
00:28:06,799 --> 00:28:11,440
widely used radionuclide in nuclear

768
00:28:08,960 --> 00:28:14,159
medicine applied in more than 90% of

769
00:28:11,440 --> 00:28:16,159
diagnostic imaging studies.

770
00:28:14,159 --> 00:28:19,039
So now you've seen the three main decay

771
00:28:16,159 --> 00:28:20,880
mechanisms alpha, beta and gamma. In the

772
00:28:19,039 --> 00:28:24,360
next step we'll discuss the final one

773
00:28:20,880 --> 00:28:24,360
electron capture.

774
00:28:25,840 --> 00:28:30,000
The final type of decay we'll discuss is

775
00:28:27,919 --> 00:28:32,159
electron capture.

776
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:34,720
In electron capture, a proton in the

777
00:28:32,159 --> 00:28:36,559
nucleus is transformed into a neutron.

778
00:28:34,720 --> 00:28:38,559
This happens when the nucleus captures

779
00:28:36,559 --> 00:28:41,039
one of the atoms own inner electrons,

780
00:28:38,559 --> 00:28:42,960
usually from the K shell.

781
00:28:41,039 --> 00:28:44,960
By capturing this electron, the proton

782
00:28:42,960 --> 00:28:47,360
combines with it to form a neutron and a

783
00:28:44,960 --> 00:28:49,440
neutrino is released.

784
00:28:47,360 --> 00:28:51,760
The daughter nucleus is often left in an

785
00:28:49,440 --> 00:28:54,880
excited state. To release that excess

786
00:28:51,760 --> 00:28:56,799
energy emits X-rays or or electrons. In

787
00:28:54,880 --> 00:28:59,039
some cases, additional radiation is

788
00:28:56,799 --> 00:29:01,200
produced through what we call breong, a

789
00:28:59,039 --> 00:29:02,720
German word meaning breaking radiation.

790
00:29:01,200 --> 00:29:04,320
This process is similar to what we

791
00:29:02,720 --> 00:29:06,080
studied earlier in X-ray production

792
00:29:04,320 --> 00:29:08,080
where electrons slow down and emit

793
00:29:06,080 --> 00:29:10,080
radiation.

794
00:29:08,080 --> 00:29:11,919
So electron capture does not directly

795
00:29:10,080 --> 00:29:14,480
produce a particle like an alpha or a

796
00:29:11,919 --> 00:29:17,039
beta. Instead it changes the balance

797
00:29:14,480 --> 00:29:19,600
inside the nucleus and produces photons

798
00:29:17,039 --> 00:29:22,240
either x-rays or gamma rays along with

799
00:29:19,600 --> 00:29:23,520
other possible emissions.

800
00:29:22,240 --> 00:29:25,279
This mechanism adds to the

801
00:29:23,520 --> 00:29:26,799
classification of decay types and it

802
00:29:25,279 --> 00:29:28,720
highlights again that photons can be

803
00:29:26,799 --> 00:29:30,799
generated in different ways. Whether

804
00:29:28,720 --> 00:29:32,799
they are called x-rays or gamma rays

805
00:29:30,799 --> 00:29:35,360
depends not on the photon itself but on

806
00:29:32,799 --> 00:29:38,080
how it was produced.

807
00:29:35,360 --> 00:29:39,279
Here

808
00:29:38,080 --> 00:29:41,679
is another way of classifying

809
00:29:39,279 --> 00:29:44,159
radioactive decay. In total, you can

810
00:29:41,679 --> 00:29:46,000
think of five types.

811
00:29:44,159 --> 00:29:48,159
First, alpha decay, which typically

812
00:29:46,000 --> 00:29:50,159
occurs among the heavier elements.

813
00:29:48,159 --> 00:29:52,559
Second, beta minus decay. Third,

814
00:29:50,159 --> 00:29:54,880
positron emission or beta plus decay.

815
00:29:52,559 --> 00:29:57,039
Fourth, electron capture. And finally,

816
00:29:54,880 --> 00:29:58,960
spontaneous vision.

817
00:29:57,039 --> 00:30:01,360
For our purposes, the first four are the

818
00:29:58,960 --> 00:30:03,279
most important. Spontaneous vision is

819
00:30:01,360 --> 00:30:05,039
more of a nuclear physics phenomenon and

820
00:30:03,279 --> 00:30:06,720
is not especially relevant to medical

821
00:30:05,039 --> 00:30:08,399
imaging.

822
00:30:06,720 --> 00:30:10,240
So depending on which textbook you

823
00:30:08,399 --> 00:30:12,320
consult, you may see these processes

824
00:30:10,240 --> 00:30:13,840
grouped slightly differently. But for

825
00:30:12,320 --> 00:30:16,080
this course, just keep in mind the

826
00:30:13,840 --> 00:30:18,720
essential ones, alpha, beta, gamma, and

827
00:30:16,080 --> 00:30:20,640
electron capture. Within beta decay,

828
00:30:18,720 --> 00:30:22,799
remember there are two cases, beta minus

829
00:30:20,640 --> 00:30:24,640
and beta plus.

830
00:30:22,799 --> 00:30:26,480
With that, you now have a solid overview

831
00:30:24,640 --> 00:30:30,440
of the main decay mechanisms we will

832
00:30:26,480 --> 00:30:30,440
encounter in nuclear imaging.

833
00:30:31,919 --> 00:30:35,600
This table lists some of the most common

834
00:30:33,679 --> 00:30:37,440
radioactive tracers used in nuclear

835
00:30:35,600 --> 00:30:39,200
medicine. Each tracer has a

836
00:30:37,440 --> 00:30:41,520
characteristic half-life in a specific

837
00:30:39,200 --> 00:30:44,559
gamma ray energy.

838
00:30:41,520 --> 00:30:47,520
For example, technium 99m has a halflife

839
00:30:44,559 --> 00:30:49,679
of about 6 hours and emits gamma rays at

840
00:30:47,520 --> 00:30:52,320
140 kilo electron volts which is ideal

841
00:30:49,679 --> 00:30:54,399
for imaging. Gallium 67 has a longer

842
