01:00:56,799 --> 01:01:02,079
across several dodes, the signal has

1655
01:00:58,799 --> 01:01:04,319
been multiplied millions of times.

1656
01:01:02,079 --> 01:01:06,319
At the final stage, what started as just

1657
01:01:04,319 --> 01:01:08,240
one or two photons of light has been

1658
01:01:06,319 --> 01:01:10,880
amplified into a strong stream of

1659
01:01:08,240 --> 01:01:12,880
electrons, a measurable current. This

1660
01:01:10,880 --> 01:01:14,720
current is then sent out of the PMT as

1661
01:01:12,880 --> 01:01:17,839
an analog signal which can later be

1662
01:01:14,720 --> 01:01:19,920
digitized for image reconstruction.

1663
01:01:17,839 --> 01:01:21,680
So the PMT performs two essential

1664
01:01:19,920 --> 01:01:23,760
functions. It converts light into

1665
01:01:21,680 --> 01:01:25,920
electrons and then it magnifies that

1666
01:01:23,760 --> 01:01:28,160
tiny signal step by step until it's

1667
01:01:25,920 --> 01:01:30,400
strong enough to record. Without this

1668
01:01:28,160 --> 01:01:32,319
amplification process, the gamma camera

1669
01:01:30,400 --> 01:01:36,359
simply wouldn't be able to detect enough

1670
01:01:32,319 --> 01:01:36,359
information to form an image.

1671
01:01:37,760 --> 01:01:41,760
Now that we know how to detect a signal,

1672
01:01:39,920 --> 01:01:44,160
the next challenge is to determine where

1673
01:01:41,760 --> 01:01:45,839
that signal came from.

1674
01:01:44,160 --> 01:01:48,079
Detection is handled by the photo

1675
01:01:45,839 --> 01:01:49,760
multiplier tubes, but localization,

1676
01:01:48,079 --> 01:01:51,680
figuring out the exact position of the

1677
01:01:49,760 --> 01:01:53,359
gamma ray interaction, is accomplished

1678
01:01:51,680 --> 01:01:55,200
by what's called the anger network,

1679
01:01:53,359 --> 01:01:57,359
named after Hal Anger, who invented the

1680
01:01:55,200 --> 01:01:59,680
gamma camera.

1681
01:01:57,359 --> 01:02:01,520
Here's the idea. The photo multiplier

1682
01:01:59,680 --> 01:02:04,079
tubes cannot be made infinitely small,

1683
01:02:01,520 --> 01:02:06,160
so they are spaced apart.

1684
01:02:04,079 --> 01:02:08,079
This means a single scintillation event,

1685
01:02:06,160 --> 01:02:09,920
a flash of light in the crystal, will

1686
01:02:08,079 --> 01:02:11,440
usually be seen by multiple tubes at

1687
01:02:09,920 --> 01:02:13,920
once, with some receiving stronger

1688
01:02:11,440 --> 01:02:16,000
signals and others weaker.

1689
01:02:13,920 --> 01:02:17,760
The anger network uses a carefully

1690
01:02:16,000 --> 01:02:19,359
designed arrangement of resistors, as

1691
01:02:17,760 --> 01:02:21,920
you see here, to spread out those

1692
01:02:19,359 --> 01:02:23,760
signals. By analyzing the weighted

1693
01:02:21,920 --> 01:02:25,599
distribution of the outputs, the system

1694
01:02:23,760 --> 01:02:27,440
can calculate the event's most likely

1695
01:02:25,599 --> 01:02:29,280
position.

1696
01:02:27,440 --> 01:02:31,680
In other words, the network acts like a

1697
01:02:29,280 --> 01:02:33,599
mathematical averaging system. If one

1698
01:02:31,680 --> 01:02:37,040
region receives stronger signals, the

1699
01:02:33,599 --> 01:02:38,880
center of activity is estimated there.

1700
01:02:37,040 --> 01:02:40,880
So although an individual photo

1701
01:02:38,880 --> 01:02:42,640
multiplier tube only gives you partial

1702
01:02:40,880 --> 01:02:44,559
information, the combination of many

1703
01:02:42,640 --> 01:02:46,240
tubes together processed through the

1704
01:02:44,559 --> 01:02:48,079
angle logic gives a precise

1705
01:02:46,240 --> 01:02:49,920
two-dimensional location of the gamma

1706
01:02:48,079 --> 01:02:52,319
ray event.

1707
01:02:49,920 --> 01:02:54,400
This way, every gammaray photon detected

1708
01:02:52,319 --> 01:02:55,839
is not only counted, but also assigned a

1709
01:02:54,400 --> 01:02:57,440
position in the image, allowing us to

1710
01:02:55,839 --> 01:03:01,799
build up a full spatial map of

1711
01:02:57,440 --> 01:03:01,799
radioactivity inside the body.

1712
01:03:03,200 --> 01:03:07,440
Now that we understand how detection and

1713
01:03:05,040 --> 01:03:09,839
localization work, let's see how they

1714
01:03:07,440 --> 01:03:13,280
come together in a full imaging system.

1715
01:03:09,839 --> 01:03:15,359
The result is called planer centigraphy.

1716
01:03:13,280 --> 01:03:17,280
Essentially, this is a two-dimensional

1717
01:03:15,359 --> 01:03:19,280
projection image much like a standard

1718
01:03:17,280 --> 01:03:21,520
X-ray radioraph, but instead of using

1719
01:03:19,280 --> 01:03:23,440
transmitted X-rays, we are mapping the

1720
01:03:21,520 --> 01:03:25,599
distribution of gamma rays emitted from

1721
01:03:23,440 --> 01:03:27,920
inside the body.

1722
01:03:25,599 --> 01:03:29,839
Here's the sequence of events. First,

1723
01:03:27,920 --> 01:03:31,599
the gamma rays from the patient pass

1724
01:03:29,839 --> 01:03:33,440
through the columator which selects only

1725
01:03:31,599 --> 01:03:36,319
those photons traveling in defined

1726
01:03:33,440 --> 01:03:38,079
directions.

1727
01:03:36,319 --> 01:03:39,839
Next, the gamma photons interact with

1728
01:03:38,079 --> 01:03:42,160
the scintillation crystal producing

1729
01:03:39,839 --> 01:03:43,760
flashes of visible light. Those light

1730
01:03:42,160 --> 01:03:45,359
signals are then converted into

1731
01:03:43,760 --> 01:03:47,440
electrical pulses by the photo

1732
01:03:45,359 --> 01:03:49,039
multiplier tubes.

1733
01:03:47,440 --> 01:03:51,200
The outputs are processed through the

1734
01:03:49,039 --> 01:03:53,280
position logic circuit which localizes

1735
01:03:51,200 --> 01:03:55,520
each detected event in the X and Y

1736
01:03:53,280 --> 01:03:57,520
directions. At the same time, the pulse

1737
01:03:55,520 --> 01:03:59,599
height analyzer examines the energy of

1738
01:03:57,520 --> 01:04:01,680
each pulse.

1739
01:03:59,599 --> 01:04:04,079
This step is important because scattered

1740
01:04:01,680 --> 01:04:05,520
photons inside the body lose energy and

1741
01:04:04,079 --> 01:04:07,680
we don't want to include them. They

1742
01:04:05,520 --> 01:04:09,440
would blur the image. By setting an

1743
01:04:07,680 --> 01:04:13,599
energy threshold, the system can reject

1744
01:04:09,440 --> 01:04:15,359
those lower energy scattered signals.

1745
01:04:13,599 --> 01:04:17,039
Finally, the gating circuit can

1746
01:04:15,359 --> 01:04:19,200
synchronize signal acquisition with

1747
01:04:17,039 --> 01:04:21,599
physiological motion such as the beating

1748
01:04:19,200 --> 01:04:23,119
heart or breathing cycle. That way, the

1749
01:04:21,599 --> 01:04:26,559
images can be captured at consistent

1750
01:04:23,119 --> 01:04:28,720
phases, reducing motion artifacts.

1751
01:04:26,559 --> 01:04:31,039
All of these components work together so

1752
01:04:28,720 --> 01:04:33,440
that each detected gamma photon is

1753
01:04:31,039 --> 01:04:35,280
accurately positioned and validated,

1754
01:04:33,440 --> 01:04:37,359
building up a sharp two-dimensional

1755
01:04:35,280 --> 01:04:40,920
image of radio tracer distribution

1756
01:04:37,359 --> 01:04:40,920
inside the patient.

1757
01:04:42,319 --> 01:04:45,760
Now, let's take a closer look at the

1758
01:04:43,920 --> 01:04:48,960
role of the pulse height analyzer by

1759
01:04:45,760 --> 01:04:50,720
examining the gammaray energy spectrum.

1760
01:04:48,960 --> 01:04:53,520
On the left you see the spectrum of

1761
01:04:50,720 --> 01:04:55,680
technium 99m when the camera is exposed

1762
01:04:53,520 --> 01:04:57,680
without a patient in front of it. The

1763
01:04:55,680 --> 01:04:59,440
dominant feature here is a sharp photo

1764
01:04:57,680 --> 01:05:01,520
peak centered around the expected

1765
01:04:59,440 --> 01:05:03,359
emission energy.

1766
01:05:01,520 --> 01:05:05,520
There are also smaller features such as

1767
01:05:03,359 --> 01:05:08,000
the iodine escape peak and signals from

1768
01:05:05,520 --> 01:05:09,520
compton scatter or lead x-rays. These

1769
01:05:08,000 --> 01:05:11,680
arise from interactions within the

1770
01:05:09,520 --> 01:05:13,440
detector itself.

1771
01:05:11,680 --> 01:05:15,119
But once you place a patient between the

1772
01:05:13,440 --> 01:05:17,680
source and the detector, the spectrum

1773
01:05:15,119 --> 01:05:19,440
changes as shown on the right. Now the

1774
01:05:17,680 --> 01:05:24,319
clean photo peak is broadened and the

1775
01:05:19,440 --> 01:05:26,160
lower energy region is filled in. Why?

1776
01:05:24,319 --> 01:05:28,319
Because inside the patient photons

1777
01:05:26,160 --> 01:05:30,319
undergo Compton scattering. Each

1778
01:05:28,319 --> 01:05:32,640
scattering event deflects the photon and

1779
01:05:30,319 --> 01:05:34,480
reduces its energy. The larger the

1780
01:05:32,640 --> 01:05:36,799
scattering angle, the lower the photon's

1781
01:05:34,480 --> 01:05:38,480
energy when it finally emerges. As a

1782
01:05:36,799 --> 01:05:40,480
result, the detector sees not only the

1783
01:05:38,480 --> 01:05:43,760
original full energy photons, but also a

1784
01:05:40,480 --> 01:05:45,200
cloud of degraded lower energy ones.

1785
01:05:43,760 --> 01:05:47,200
This is a problem because those

1786
01:05:45,200 --> 01:05:49,039
scattered photons don't carry accurate

1787
01:05:47,200 --> 01:05:51,440
positional information. They blur the

1788
01:05:49,039 --> 01:05:53,760
image. The solution is to use the pulse

1789
01:05:51,440 --> 01:05:55,599
height analyzer. Since the amplitude of

1790
01:05:53,760 --> 01:05:57,440
the electronic signal from the photo

1791
01:05:55,599 --> 01:05:59,760
multiplier tube is proportional to the

1792
01:05:57,440 --> 01:06:01,520
photon's energy, we can set acceptance

1793
01:05:59,760 --> 01:06:03,599
windows.

1794
01:06:01,520 --> 01:06:05,599
Only signals that fall within a narrow

1795
01:06:03,599 --> 01:06:08,880
energy range around the true photo peak,

1796
01:06:05,599 --> 01:06:11,839
for example, around 140 keV for technium

1797
01:06:08,880 --> 01:06:13,440
99M are accepted. Signals that are too

1798
01:06:11,839 --> 01:06:16,079
low, likely coming from scattered

1799
01:06:13,440 --> 01:06:17,839
photons, are rejected.

1800
01:06:16,079 --> 01:06:19,680
This energy discrimination is what gives

1801
01:06:17,839 --> 01:06:21,920
nuclear imaging its clarity, much like

1802
01:06:19,680 --> 01:06:23,520
how anti-catter grids improve X-ray

1803
01:06:21,920 --> 01:06:25,520
imaging.

1804
01:06:23,520 --> 01:06:27,119
By gating and filtering the signals in

1805
01:06:25,520 --> 01:06:28,799
this way, we make sure that the image is

1806
01:06:27,119 --> 01:06:30,960
formed primarily from primary

1807
01:06:28,799 --> 01:06:34,839
unscattered photons, preserving both

1808
01:06:30,960 --> 01:06:34,839
resolution and contrast.

1809
01:06:36,400 --> 01:06:40,000
The point spread function is a useful

1810
01:06:38,160 --> 01:06:42,559
way to judge how well a gamma camera can

1811
01:06:40,000 --> 01:06:44,319
resolve detail. The narrower the point

1812
01:06:42,559 --> 01:06:46,319
spread function, the better the spatial

1813
01:06:44,319 --> 01:06:48,079
resolution. Now, what actually

1814
01:06:46,319 --> 01:06:49,839
determines resolution in nuclear

1815
01:06:48,079 --> 01:06:51,359
imaging?

1816
01:06:49,839 --> 01:06:53,200
It turns out there are several factors

1817
01:06:51,359 --> 01:06:55,119
at play, each introducing its own kind

1818
01:06:53,200 --> 01:06:56,799
of blurring.

1819
01:06:55,119 --> 01:06:58,960
First, there's the intrinsic resolution

1820
01:06:56,799 --> 01:07:00,720
of the gamma camera itself. This is

1821
01:06:58,960 --> 01:07:02,319
limited by the scintillation crystal and

1822
01:07:00,720 --> 01:07:04,400
the electronics that localize the

1823
01:07:02,319 --> 01:07:06,319
signal. A thicker crystal spreads the

1824
01:07:04,400 --> 01:07:10,640
light more broadly and that uncertainty

1825
01:07:06,319 --> 01:07:12,720
shows up as blur in the final image.

1826
01:07:10,640 --> 01:07:15,119
Second, the geometry of the columator

1827
01:07:12,720 --> 01:07:17,440
plays a major role. Whether you use a

1828
01:07:15,119 --> 01:07:19,680
parallel hole, converging, diverging, or

1829
01:07:17,440 --> 01:07:21,680
pinhole design, the length and diameter

1830
01:07:19,680 --> 01:07:24,799
of the channels directly control how

1831
01:07:21,680 --> 01:07:26,559
sharp or blurry the image will be.

1832
01:07:24,799 --> 01:07:28,480
Longer, narrower channels improve

1833
01:07:26,559 --> 01:07:30,480
resolution but reduce sensitivity

1834
01:07:28,480 --> 01:07:32,079
because many photons are blocked.

1835
01:07:30,480 --> 01:07:33,920
Shorter, wider channels improve

1836
01:07:32,079 --> 01:07:35,599
sensitivity, but at the cost of blurrier

1837
01:07:33,920 --> 01:07:37,280
images.

1838
01:07:35,599 --> 01:07:38,559
Third, Compton scattering inside the

1839
01:07:37,280 --> 01:07:41,119
patient adds another layer of

1840
01:07:38,559 --> 01:07:42,960
uncertainty. The deeper the radioactive

1841
01:07:41,119 --> 01:07:44,960
source lies within the body, the more

1842
01:07:42,960 --> 01:07:47,440
gamma photons are likely to scatter

1843
01:07:44,960 --> 01:07:49,359
before they escape.

1844
01:07:47,440 --> 01:07:51,200
Scattered photons are misleading. They

1845
01:07:49,359 --> 01:07:54,160
point in the wrong direction, lowering

1846
01:07:51,200 --> 01:07:55,920
contrast and degrading resolution.

1847
01:07:54,160 --> 01:07:57,920
When we put all these effects together,

1848
01:07:55,920 --> 01:08:00,319
the total system resolution can be

1849
01:07:57,920 --> 01:08:01,839
expressed mathematically. Just as when

1850
01:08:00,319 --> 01:08:03,760
you combine independent sources of

1851
01:08:01,839 --> 01:08:05,440
random error, you add their variances

1852
01:08:03,760 --> 01:08:07,520
here too. The variances of the three

1853
01:08:05,440 --> 01:08:09,280
components intrinsic resolution,

1854
01:08:07,520 --> 01:08:11,440
columator resolution, and compton

1855
01:08:09,280 --> 01:08:13,039
scatter add together. The overall

1856
01:08:11,440 --> 01:08:16,000
resolution is then the square root of

1857
01:08:13,039 --> 01:08:18,319
that sum. In other words,

1858
01:08:16,000 --> 01:08:20,159
system resolution equals the square root

1859
01:08:18,319 --> 01:08:22,640
of the sum of the squares of the three

1860
01:08:20,159 --> 01:08:24,719
main contributions.

1861
01:08:22,640 --> 01:08:26,880
This is the fundamental idea. Every

1862
01:08:24,719 --> 01:08:28,640
component of the imaging chain blurs the

1863
01:08:26,880 --> 01:08:30,400
signal a little bit and together they

1864
01:08:28,640 --> 01:08:35,480
define the ultimate sharpness or

1865
01:08:30,400 --> 01:08:35,480
fuzziness of the nuclear medicine image.

1866
01:08:36,000 --> 01:08:40,080
When we think about the point spread

1867
01:08:37,600 --> 01:08:42,239
function or PSF, we are asking how does

1868
01:08:40,080 --> 01:08:45,960
the imaging system represent a single

1869
01:08:42,239 --> 01:08:45,960
point source of radiation.

1870
01:08:46,000 --> 01:08:49,600
Ideally, if you had a perfect gamma

1871
01:08:47,759 --> 01:08:51,600
camera, one point of activity in the

1872
01:08:49,600 --> 01:08:53,600
patient would appear as one sharp point

1873
01:08:51,600 --> 01:08:55,279
in the image.

1874
01:08:53,600 --> 01:08:57,040
In practice, because of the intrinsic

1875
01:08:55,279 --> 01:08:58,719
resolution of the scintillation crystal,

1876
01:08:57,040 --> 01:09:00,719
the photo multiplier tubes and the

1877
01:08:58,719 --> 01:09:02,560
electronics, the point source becomes

1878
01:09:00,719 --> 01:09:04,960
blurred into a Gaussian shaped

1879
01:09:02,560 --> 01:09:06,480
distribution. That Gaussian curve is

1880
01:09:04,960 --> 01:09:09,120
what you would expect from the use of

1881
01:09:06,480 --> 01:09:10,799
the camera and columator.

1882
01:09:09,120 --> 01:09:12,799
However, once you place the patient

1883
01:09:10,799 --> 01:09:15,440
between the tracer and the detector,

1884
01:09:12,799 --> 01:09:17,359
Compton scattering adds a complication.

1885
01:09:15,440 --> 01:09:19,120
Many photons scatter inside the body

1886
01:09:17,359 --> 01:09:20,640
before reaching the detector, and those

1887
01:09:19,120 --> 01:09:22,640
scattered photons broaden the

1888
01:09:20,640 --> 01:09:24,560
distribution.

1889
01:09:22,640 --> 01:09:26,640
Instead of a neat Gaussian, the PSF

1890
01:09:24,560 --> 01:09:28,480
develops a long tail. Photons detected

1891
01:09:26,640 --> 01:09:30,239
in places far from the true source

1892
01:09:28,480 --> 01:09:32,159
location.

1893
01:09:30,239 --> 01:09:34,319
This long tail is undesirable because it

1894
01:09:32,159 --> 01:09:36,400
reduces spatial resolution and creates

1895
01:09:34,319 --> 01:09:38,400
image blur. That is why we use

1896
01:09:36,400 --> 01:09:40,319
techniques like pulse height analysis to

1897
01:09:38,400 --> 01:09:42,960
reject scattered photons keeping only

1898
01:09:40,319 --> 01:09:44,560
the primary gamma rays.

1899
01:09:42,960 --> 01:09:46,960
So the main lesson here is the

1900
01:09:44,560 --> 01:09:49,040
Gaussian-like PSF represents the best

1901
01:09:46,960 --> 01:09:51,279
case resolution of the gamma camera, but

1902
01:09:49,040 --> 01:09:53,120
Compton scattering stretches the PSF,

1903
01:09:51,279 --> 01:09:55,199
adding tails and degrading image

1904
01:09:53,120 --> 01:09:57,120
quality. Understanding and controlling

1905
01:09:55,199 --> 01:10:00,920
this spread is fundamental to improving

1906
01:09:57,120 --> 01:10:00,920
nuclear medicine imaging.

1907
01:10:02,400 --> 01:10:05,679
Now let's look at another important

1908
01:10:03,920 --> 01:10:07,440
issue in nuclear imaging systems, dead

1909
01:10:05,679 --> 01:10:09,280
bedtime.

1910
01:10:07,440 --> 01:10:10,880
When a gamma photon interacts with the

1911
01:10:09,280 --> 01:10:13,600
scintillation crystal, it produces a

1912
01:10:10,880 --> 01:10:15,360
pulse of light. That pulse has a finite

1913
01:10:13,600 --> 01:10:17,199
width. It takes some recovery time

1914
01:10:15,360 --> 01:10:19,760
before the system is ready to register

1915
01:10:17,199 --> 01:10:21,840
the next event. If two or more photons

1916
01:10:19,760 --> 01:10:24,640
arrive too close together in time, the

1917
01:10:21,840 --> 01:10:26,560
system cannot distinguish them.

1918
01:10:24,640 --> 01:10:28,719
Instead of recording two events, it may

1919
01:10:26,560 --> 01:10:30,880
only record one or in some cases none at

1920
01:10:28,719 --> 01:10:32,960
all. This limitation is called dead

1921
01:10:30,880 --> 01:10:34,719
time. It reflects the fact that every

1922
01:10:32,960 --> 01:10:37,360
detector and its electronics need a

1923
01:10:34,719 --> 01:10:39,440
short period to reset after each event.

1924
01:10:37,360 --> 01:10:41,360
If the incoming rate of photons is too

1925
01:10:39,440 --> 01:10:44,320
high, the detector simply cannot keep

1926
01:10:41,360 --> 01:10:46,480
up. Think of it like catching candies

1927
01:10:44,320 --> 01:10:48,159
falling from the ceiling. If they fall

1928
01:10:46,480 --> 01:10:50,320
slowly, you can catch and count them one

1929
01:10:48,159 --> 01:10:51,760
by one. But if they start raining down

1930
01:10:50,320 --> 01:10:54,000
too quickly, you can't separate them

1931
01:10:51,760 --> 01:10:58,000
anymore. You miss some. That's exactly

1932
01:10:54,000 --> 01:10:59,679
what happens here with gamma photons.

1933
01:10:58,000 --> 01:11:01,760
Mathematically, we can describe this

1934
01:10:59,679 --> 01:11:03,920
with the true count rate denoted by

1935
01:11:01,760 --> 01:11:06,159
capital N, which is the number of gamma

1936
01:11:03,920 --> 01:11:07,520
interactions that really happen. Then

1937
01:11:06,159 --> 01:11:09,280
there's the observed count rate,

1938
01:11:07,520 --> 01:11:11,040
lowercase N, which is what the detector

1939
01:11:09,280 --> 01:11:14,159
actually records. Because of dead

1940
01:11:11,040 --> 01:11:16,400
bedtime losses, N is always less than N.

1941
01:11:14,159 --> 01:11:18,480
The system dead time, usually written as

1942
01:11:16,400 --> 01:11:20,400
the Greek letter toao, can be expressed

1943
01:11:18,480 --> 01:11:25,760
as the difference between the reciprocal

1944
01:11:20,400 --> 01:11:27,600
of these two rates. TOAO= 1 / NUS 1 /n.

1945
01:11:25,760 --> 01:11:29,280
This formula tells us how much recovery

1946
01:11:27,600 --> 01:11:31,360
time the system effectively needs

1947
01:11:29,280 --> 01:11:33,040
between two detected events. In

1948
01:11:31,360 --> 01:11:34,800
practical gamma cameras, dead bedtime

1949
01:11:33,040 --> 01:11:36,640
losses are usually not a big problem

1950
01:11:34,800 --> 01:11:38,640
unless the injected activity is very

1951
01:11:36,640 --> 01:11:42,280
high, but it's an important limitation

1952
01:11:38,640 --> 01:11:42,280
to be aware of.

1953
01:11:43,679 --> 01:11:46,719
Now, let's connect what we have learned

1954
01:11:45,360 --> 01:11:49,440
to one of the most important

1955
01:11:46,719 --> 01:11:51,679
applications of nuclear imaging, FDG PET

1956
01:11:49,440 --> 01:11:53,520
for cancer imaging.

1957
01:11:51,679 --> 01:11:56,640
PET stands for posatron emission

1958
01:11:53,520 --> 01:11:58,560
tomography. Unlike CT or MRI which give

1959
01:11:56,640 --> 01:12:00,800
us structural information, PET provides

1960
01:11:58,560 --> 01:12:02,960
functional imaging. It shows us how

1961
01:12:00,800 --> 01:12:06,640
tissues are working metabolically inside

1962
01:12:02,960 --> 01:12:08,800
the body. The key is the tracer we use

1963
01:12:06,640 --> 01:12:12,080
for PET. The most widely used tracer is

1964
01:12:08,800 --> 01:12:13,920
fluoroxy glucose or FDG. This is a

1965
01:12:12,080 --> 01:12:16,400
glucose analog that is produced in a

1966
01:12:13,920 --> 01:12:18,239
cyclron. Because FDG behaves like

1967
01:12:16,400 --> 01:12:20,560
glucose, it is taken up by tissues

1968
01:12:18,239 --> 01:12:22,320
according to their metabolic activity.

1969
01:12:20,560 --> 01:12:24,400
Cancer cells are often much more

1970
01:12:22,320 --> 01:12:27,280
metabolically active than normal cells.

1971
01:12:24,400 --> 01:12:29,760
So they absorb more FDG.

1972
01:12:27,280 --> 01:12:31,600
When we image the concentration of FDG,

1973
01:12:29,760 --> 01:12:34,400
we are essentially mapping the regions

1974
01:12:31,600 --> 01:12:36,880
of high glucose metabolism. This makes

1975
01:12:34,400 --> 01:12:39,600
PET a very powerful tool for detecting

1976
01:12:36,880 --> 01:12:42,159
tumors and also for evaluating whether

1977
01:12:39,600 --> 01:12:45,120
cancer has metastasized that is spread

1978
01:12:42,159 --> 01:12:47,280
to other sites in the body.

1979
01:12:45,120 --> 01:12:50,320
The physics behind PET is tied to beta

1980
01:12:47,280 --> 01:12:52,640
plus decay. FDG emits a positron which

1981
01:12:50,320 --> 01:12:54,800
quickly encounters a nearby electron.

1982
01:12:52,640 --> 01:12:56,640
When the posetron and electron meet,

1983
01:12:54,800 --> 01:12:58,960
they annihilate producing a pair of

1984
01:12:56,640 --> 01:13:01,440
gamma photons moving in nearly opposite

1985
01:12:58,960 --> 01:13:03,199
directions. These paired photons are

1986
01:13:01,440 --> 01:13:04,880
detected simultaneously. That's the

1987
01:13:03,199 --> 01:13:06,640
coincidence detection we discussed

1988
01:13:04,880 --> 01:13:08,960
earlier.

1989
01:13:06,640 --> 01:13:10,880
This entire principle posetron emission,

1990
01:13:08,960 --> 01:13:13,280
annihilation, and detection of the

1991
01:13:10,880 --> 01:13:15,840
paired photons forms the physical and

1992
01:13:13,280 --> 01:13:19,360
chemical foundation of PET imaging.

1993
01:13:15,840 --> 01:13:22,000
Today, FDG PET accounts for about 90% of

1994
01:13:19,360 --> 01:13:24,000
all PET scans, highlighting just how

1995
01:13:22,000 --> 01:13:28,280
central this method is in cancer

1996
01:13:24,000 --> 01:13:28,280
diagnosis and treatment planning.

1997
01:13:29,840 --> 01:13:33,120
Now, let's take a closer look at how

1998
01:13:31,440 --> 01:13:34,719
coincidence detection works in PET

1999
01:13:33,120 --> 01:13:36,719
imaging.

2000
01:13:34,719 --> 01:13:38,640
When a posetron emitted from the tracer

2001
01:13:36,719 --> 01:13:41,199
annihilates with an electron, the

2002
01:13:38,640 --> 01:13:44,400
process produces two gamma photons, each

2003
01:13:41,199 --> 01:13:46,640
with an energy of 51 kilo electron volts

2004
01:13:44,400 --> 01:13:48,560
traveling in nearly opposite directions.

2005
01:13:46,640 --> 01:13:50,640
The key is that both of these photons

2006
01:13:48,560 --> 01:13:54,000
need to be detected at the same time to

2007
01:13:50,640 --> 01:13:55,920
confirm a true event.

2008
01:13:54,000 --> 01:13:58,800
Here's how the process works step by

2009
01:13:55,920 --> 01:14:01,440
step. Each gamma photon first enters a

2010
01:13:58,800 --> 01:14:03,520
detector crystal. Inside the crystal,

2011
01:14:01,440 --> 01:14:06,159
the photon interacts and produces

2012
01:14:03,520 --> 01:14:08,880
visible light which is then amplified by

2013
01:14:06,159 --> 01:14:11,280
a photo multiplier tube, giving us an

2014
01:14:08,880 --> 01:14:13,199
electronic pulse.

2015
01:14:11,280 --> 01:14:14,960
But not every pulse comes from the

2016
01:14:13,199 --> 01:14:17,040
correct kind of photon. Scattered

2017
01:14:14,960 --> 01:14:19,199
photons, for example, may have lower

2018
01:14:17,040 --> 01:14:21,679
energy. This is where the pulse height

2019
01:14:19,199 --> 01:14:23,920
analyzer comes in. It checks the energy

2020
01:14:21,679 --> 01:14:25,760
of each detected photon and only accepts

2021
01:14:23,920 --> 01:14:29,280
those within a narrow energy window

2022
01:14:25,760 --> 01:14:31,440
around 511 kV. This helps us reject

2023
01:14:29,280 --> 01:14:33,280
scattered signals.

2024
01:14:31,440 --> 01:14:35,760
Now imagine we have two detectors on

2025
01:14:33,280 --> 01:14:37,840
opposite sides. If one detector

2026
01:14:35,760 --> 01:14:39,920
registers a photon and the other also

2027
01:14:37,840 --> 01:14:41,920
registers a photon of the correct energy

2028
01:14:39,920 --> 01:14:44,400
and the two signals arrive within a very

2029
01:14:41,920 --> 01:14:46,000
short time window just a few nanconds

2030
01:14:44,400 --> 01:14:48,320
apart then the coincidence circuit

2031
01:14:46,000 --> 01:14:49,679
recognizes them as a pair. This means

2032
01:14:48,320 --> 01:14:51,679
they must have come from the same

2033
01:14:49,679 --> 01:14:55,640
annihilation event along the line

2034
01:14:51,679 --> 01:14:55,640
connecting the two detectors.

2035
01:14:55,840 --> 01:15:00,800
So only when both detectors see valid

2036
01:14:58,159 --> 01:15:02,800
511 KV photons at nearly the same

2037
01:15:00,800 --> 01:15:04,560
instant do we count it as a true PET

2038
01:15:02,800 --> 01:15:06,800
event.

2039
01:15:04,560 --> 01:15:08,880
This is what allows PET imaging to trace

2040
01:15:06,800 --> 01:15:10,640
the exact line of response inside the

2041
01:15:08,880 --> 01:15:12,400
body leading to the powerful

2042
01:15:10,640 --> 01:15:14,320
three-dimensional reconstructions that

2043
01:15:12,400 --> 01:15:17,719
make PET such a valuable tool in

2044
01:15:14,320 --> 01:15:17,719
clinical imaging.

2045
01:15:19,199 --> 01:15:23,679
Now here's another way to visualize

2046
01:15:21,040 --> 01:15:25,520
coincidence detection. In this diagram,

2047
01:15:23,679 --> 01:15:27,679
we see detectors arranged around the

2048
01:15:25,520 --> 01:15:30,960
patient with a posetron annihilation

2049
01:15:27,679 --> 01:15:32,960
event taking place inside the body.

2050
01:15:30,960 --> 01:15:36,320
From this event, two gamma photons are

2051
01:15:32,960 --> 01:15:38,239
emitted in nearly opposite directions.

2052
01:15:36,320 --> 01:15:40,560
Each detector continuously records

2053
01:15:38,239 --> 01:15:42,159
signals as a function of time. Most of

2054
01:15:40,560 --> 01:15:44,880
the time, what you see are background

2055
01:15:42,159 --> 01:15:47,040
signals or small pulses. But every now

2056
01:15:44,880 --> 01:15:49,440
and then, a true gamma photon from an

2057
01:15:47,040 --> 01:15:52,480
annihilation event strikes the detector,

2058
01:15:49,440 --> 01:15:54,159
and this produces a sharp pulse.

2059
01:15:52,480 --> 01:15:56,800
The key idea is that we are not looking

2060
01:15:54,159 --> 01:15:58,400
at a single detector alone. Instead, we

2061
01:15:56,800 --> 01:16:01,520
compare signals across pairs of

2062
01:15:58,400 --> 01:16:03,199
detectors. If detector Y and detector J

2063
01:16:01,520 --> 01:16:04,800
located opposite one another both

2064
01:16:03,199 --> 01:16:06,640
register a pulse at nearly the same

2065
01:16:04,800 --> 01:16:09,679
instant and if both pulses have the

2066
01:16:06,640 --> 01:16:11,440
correct energy around 511 KV, then we

2067
01:16:09,679 --> 01:16:13,360
know with high confidence that a true

2068
01:16:11,440 --> 01:16:17,360
annihilation event occurred along the

2069
01:16:13,360 --> 01:16:19,280
line connecting those two detectors.

2070
01:16:17,360 --> 01:16:21,920
So, the system continuously searches for

2071
01:16:19,280 --> 01:16:23,840
these paired pulses. The electronics are

2072
01:16:21,920 --> 01:16:26,159
designed to be fast and complex enough

2073
01:16:23,840 --> 01:16:28,159
to check every possible detector pair in

2074
01:16:26,159 --> 01:16:30,480
real time.

2075
01:16:28,159 --> 01:16:32,400
Whenever a coincidence is confirmed, the

2076
01:16:30,480 --> 01:16:35,199
system draws what we call a line of

2077
01:16:32,400 --> 01:16:37,199
response between the two detectors. That

2078
01:16:35,199 --> 01:16:39,199
line is where the annihilation must have

2079
01:16:37,199 --> 01:16:41,360
taken place.

2080
01:16:39,199 --> 01:16:43,360
This principle detecting paired pulses

2081
01:16:41,360 --> 01:16:45,920
and building lines of response is the

2082
01:16:43,360 --> 01:16:48,159
foundation of PET imaging. By collecting

2083
01:16:45,920 --> 01:16:50,400
many such lines from annihilations

2084
01:16:48,159 --> 01:16:52,880
throughout the body, we can reconstruct

2085
01:16:50,400 --> 01:16:57,320
a full three-dimensional image of tracer

2086
01:16:52,880 --> 01:16:57,320
distribution inside the patient.

2087
01:16:58,880 --> 01:17:03,360
Up to now, we've said that coincidence

2088
01:17:01,040 --> 01:17:05,280
detection tells us that an annihilation

2089
01:17:03,360 --> 01:17:08,640
event must have occurred somewhere along

2090
01:17:05,280 --> 01:17:09,920
the line connecting two detectors,

2091
01:17:08,640 --> 01:17:11,679
but that's still quite a lot of

2092
01:17:09,920 --> 01:17:13,440
uncertainty. It could be anywhere on

2093
01:17:11,679 --> 01:17:15,440
that line.

2094
01:17:13,440 --> 01:17:18,080
Now with time offlight detection we can

2095
01:17:15,440 --> 01:17:20,159
do better. The idea is straightforward.

2096
01:17:18,080 --> 01:17:22,239
When the two gamma photons are produced

2097
01:17:20,159 --> 01:17:24,239
they leave the annihilation site at the

2098
01:17:22,239 --> 01:17:27,040
same instant traveling in opposite

2099
01:17:24,239 --> 01:17:29,280
directions at the speed of light.

2100
01:17:27,040 --> 01:17:31,120
If the annihilation happens exactly in

2101
01:17:29,280 --> 01:17:33,520
the middle the photons will reach both

2102
01:17:31,120 --> 01:17:35,360
detectors at the same time. But if the

2103
01:17:33,520 --> 01:17:37,199
event happens closer to one detector,

2104
01:17:35,360 --> 01:17:39,199
the photon on that side will arrive

2105
01:17:37,199 --> 01:17:41,440
slightly earlier.

2106
01:17:39,199 --> 01:17:43,120
So by measuring this tiny arrival time

2107
01:17:41,440 --> 01:17:45,520
difference, we can estimate where along

2108
01:17:43,120 --> 01:17:47,360
the line the event occurred. Of course,

2109
01:17:45,520 --> 01:17:49,600
because our timing measurements are not

2110
01:17:47,360 --> 01:17:51,520
infinitely precise, there is still some

2111
01:17:49,600 --> 01:17:53,600
uncertainty. That's why we don't

2112
01:17:51,520 --> 01:17:56,080
pinpoint a single location, but instead

2113
01:17:53,600 --> 01:17:58,080
assign a probability distribution

2114
01:17:56,080 --> 01:18:02,159
centered closer to one detector or the

2115
01:17:58,080 --> 01:18:04,640
other depending on the measured delay.

2116
01:18:02,159 --> 01:18:07,040
The effect is illustrated here. Without

2117
01:18:04,640 --> 01:18:09,040
timelight information, the whole line is

2118
01:18:07,040 --> 01:18:11,360
equally likely. So the back projected

2119
01:18:09,040 --> 01:18:13,199
signal is spread out. With time of

2120
01:18:11,360 --> 01:18:15,360
flight, the probability is concentrated

2121
01:18:13,199 --> 01:18:17,040
in a smaller region along that line.

2122
01:18:15,360 --> 01:18:18,800
When you combine millions of such

2123
01:18:17,040 --> 01:18:21,040
measurements, the reconstructed PET

2124
01:18:18,800 --> 01:18:24,239
image is sharper with better contrast

2125
01:18:21,040 --> 01:18:26,239
and less noise.

2126
01:18:24,239 --> 01:18:28,480
So the concept is simple. Coincidence

2127
01:18:26,239 --> 01:18:30,640
detection gives us the line and time of

2128
01:18:28,480 --> 01:18:32,320
flight narrows it down to a region on

2129
01:18:30,640 --> 01:18:34,000
that line where the paired emission

2130
01:18:32,320 --> 01:18:36,000
happened.

2131
01:18:34,000 --> 01:18:37,679
This is why modern PET scanners

2132
01:18:36,000 --> 01:18:40,400
increasingly use time offlight

2133
01:18:37,679 --> 01:18:43,120
technology. It significantly improves

2134
01:18:40,400 --> 01:18:46,760
image quality without requiring extra

2135
01:18:43,120 --> 01:18:46,760
radiation dose.

